IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `nz-interpretation-time`

-- Tests for nz-interpretation-time.l4.
-- Every expected value below was decided by reading the section quoted beside it (or the named
-- calendar fact) BEFORE the module was run; none is copied from the module's output.
-- Weekdays and the dates of the seven named days are facts of the calendar, not of the Act: the Act
-- dates none of them, so each test supplies them as the calendar input. Where a test says "Saturday" the
-- weekday was read off a printed calendar.

§ `Calendars supplied as inputs (not from the Act)`

-- 2026: Waitangi Day Fri 6 Feb; Good Friday 3 Apr; Easter Monday 6 Apr; Anzac Day Sat 25 Apr;
-- the Sovereign's birthday Mon 1 Jun; Matariki Observance Day Fri 10 Jul; Labour Day Mon 26 Oct.
GIVETH A LIST OF `Named day`
`the calendar for 2026` MEANS
    LIST (`Named day` OF `Waitangi Day`, (Date 6 2 2026))
       , (`Named day` OF `Good Friday`, (Date 3 4 2026))
       , (`Named day` OF `Easter Monday`, (Date 6 4 2026))
       , (`Named day` OF `Anzac Day`, (Date 25 4 2026))
       , (`Named day` OF `the Sovereign's birthday`, (Date 1 6 2026))
       , (`Named day` OF `Matariki Observance Day`, (Date 10 7 2026))
       , (`Named day` OF `Labour Day`, (Date 26 10 2026))

-- 2027: Waitangi Day Sat 6 Feb; Good Friday 26 Mar; Easter Monday 29 Mar; Anzac Day Sun 25 Apr;
-- the Sovereign's birthday Mon 7 Jun; Matariki Observance Day Fri 25 Jun; Labour Day Mon 25 Oct.
GIVETH A LIST OF `Named day`
`the calendar for 2027` MEANS
    LIST (`Named day` OF `Waitangi Day`, (Date 6 2 2027))
       , (`Named day` OF `Good Friday`, (Date 26 3 2027))
       , (`Named day` OF `Easter Monday`, (Date 29 3 2027))
       , (`Named day` OF `Anzac Day`, (Date 25 4 2027))
       , (`Named day` OF `the Sovereign's birthday`, (Date 7 6 2027))
       , (`Named day` OF `Matariki Observance Day`, (Date 25 6 2027))
       , (`Named day` OF `Labour Day`, (Date 25 10 2027))

-- 2024: Waitangi Day Tue 6 Feb; Good Friday 29 Mar; Easter Monday 1 Apr; Anzac Day Thu 25 Apr;
-- the Sovereign's birthday Mon 3 Jun; Matariki Observance Day Fri 28 Jun; Labour Day Mon 28 Oct.
GIVETH A LIST OF `Named day`
`the calendar for 2024` MEANS
    LIST (`Named day` OF `Waitangi Day`, (Date 6 2 2024))
       , (`Named day` OF `Good Friday`, (Date 29 3 2024))
       , (`Named day` OF `Easter Monday`, (Date 1 4 2024))
       , (`Named day` OF `Anzac Day`, (Date 25 4 2024))
       , (`Named day` OF `the Sovereign's birthday`, (Date 3 6 2024))
       , (`Named day` OF `Matariki Observance Day`, (Date 28 6 2024))
       , (`Named day` OF `Labour Day`, (Date 28 10 2024))

GIVETH A LIST OF `Named day`
`the calendars for 2026 and 2027` MEANS append `the calendar for 2026` `the calendar for 2027`

§ `Calendar arithmetic that is not law`

-- Printed calendar: 5 Jan 2026 Monday; 31 Jan 2026 Saturday; 1 Feb 2026 Sunday; 29 Feb 2024 Thursday; 31 Dec 2025 Wednesday.
#ASSERT `the weekday number of` (Date 5 1 2026) EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the weekday number of` (Date 31 1 2026) EQUALS 5
#ASSERT `the weekday number of` (Date 1 2 2026) EQUALS 6
#ASSERT `the weekday number of` (Date 29 2 2024) EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `the weekday number of` (Date 31 12 2025) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `is the first of January` (Date 1 1 2026)
#ASSERT NOT (`is the first of January` (Date 1 2 2026))

§ `Section 13 -- "month" and "working day"`

-- src:127 | month means a calendar month
-- s 13: "month means a calendar month".
#ASSERT `the Act's meaning of a month` EQUALS "a calendar month"

-- limb (a): a Saturday and a Sunday are not working days; an ordinary Monday to Friday is.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 31 1 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 1 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 2 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 10 3 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- limb (a): the named days. Waitangi Day (Fri), Good Friday, Easter Monday, the Sovereign's birthday, Matariki, Labour Day.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 6 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 3 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 6 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 1 6 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 10 7 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 26 10 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- the day before and the day after a named day are working days (boundary on each side).
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 5 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 7 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- limb (e): Anzac Day 2026 falls on Saturday 25 April, so the following Monday 27 April is not a working day; Tuesday 28 April is.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 25 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 27 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 28 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- limb (e): Waitangi Day 2027 falls on Saturday 6 February, so Monday 8 February is not a working day; Tuesday 9 February is.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 8 2 2027) `according to` `the calendar for 2027` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 9 2 2027) `according to` `the calendar for 2027` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- limb (e): Anzac Day 2027 falls on Sunday 25 April, so Monday 26 April is not a working day; Tuesday 27 April is.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 26 4 2027) `according to` `the calendar for 2027` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 27 4 2027) `according to` `the calendar for 2027` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- limb (e) does not apply when the named day falls on a weekday: Monday 9 Feb 2026 follows Waitangi Day (Fri 6 Feb), and is a working day.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 9 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- limb (b): 25 December to 2 January inclusive, both sides of each edge. Thursday 24 Dec 2026 is a working day.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 24 12 2026) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 25 12 2026) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 28 12 2026) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 31 12 2026) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 1 1 2027) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 2 1 2027) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- limb (c): 1 January 2027 is a Friday, so Monday 4 January 2027 is not a working day; Tuesday 5 January 2027 is.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 4 1 2027) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 5 1 2027) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- limb (c) with 1 January 2021 a Friday: Monday 4 January 2021 is not a working day (decided with no calendar at all).
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 4 1 2021) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- limb (d): 1 January 2022 is a Saturday, so Monday 3 and Tuesday 4 January 2022 are not working days; Wednesday 5 is not decided without a calendar.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 3 1 2022) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 4 1 2022) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 5 1 2022) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS NOTHING
-- limb (d): 1 January 2023 is a Sunday, so Monday 2 (also inside the 25 December to 2 January period) and Tuesday 3 January 2023 are not working days.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 2 1 2023) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 3 1 2023) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- No calendar for the year: the answer needs a named day the Act does not date, so NOTHING.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 2 2 2026) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 2 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2027` EQUALS NOTHING
-- A weekend is a weekend whatever the calendar: no calendar is needed.
#ASSERT `is a working day` (Date 1 2 2026) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Section 17 -- sending by post`

GIVETH A `Posting facts`
`a letter posted in the ordinary way` MEANS `Posting facts` OF TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 5 3 2026)

-- src:178 | 17 When sending by post is taken as done
-- src:179 | This section applies to legislation that authorises or requires a document to be sent by post (whether the expression serve, give, or send, or any other expression, is used).
-- src:180 | The document is taken to be sent if it is properly addressed, prepaid, and posted as a postal article.
-- src:181 | The document is taken to be received at the time at which it would be delivered in the ordinary course of post.
-- src:182 | However, subsection (2) or (3) does not apply if the contrary is proved.
-- src:183 | See also Part 4 of the Contract and Commercial Law Act 2017, which provides for the deemed timing and receipt of electronic communications.
-- (2) sent if properly addressed, prepaid and posted; (3) received when it would be delivered in the ordinary course of post; (4) unless the contrary is proved.
#ASSERT `whether the document is taken to be sent` `a letter posted in the ordinary way` EQUALS `Taken to have been sent`
#ASSERT `the day the document is taken to be received` `a letter posted in the ordinary way` EQUALS JUST (Date 5 3 2026)
-- Each condition missing in turn: not taken to be sent, and no deemed receipt.
#ASSERT `whether the document is taken to be sent` (`Posting facts` OF TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 5 3 2026)) EQUALS `Not taken to have been sent by section 17`
#ASSERT `whether the document is taken to be sent` (`Posting facts` OF TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 5 3 2026)) EQUALS `Not taken to have been sent by section 17`
#ASSERT `whether the document is taken to be sent` (`Posting facts` OF TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 5 3 2026)) EQUALS `Not taken to have been sent by section 17`
#ASSERT `the day the document is taken to be received` (`Posting facts` OF TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 5 3 2026)) EQUALS NOTHING
-- (1) the section applies only to legislation that authorises or requires sending by post.
#ASSERT `whether the document is taken to be sent` (`Posting facts` OF FALSE, TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, (Date 5 3 2026)) EQUALS `Not taken to have been sent by section 17`
-- (4) the contrary proved as to the sending removes the deeming of sending; as to the receipt, only the deeming of receipt.
#ASSERT `whether the document is taken to be sent` (`Posting facts` OF TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, (Date 5 3 2026)) EQUALS `Not taken to have been sent by section 17`
#ASSERT `whether the document is taken to be sent` (`Posting facts` OF TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, (Date 5 3 2026)) EQUALS `Taken to have been sent`
#ASSERT `the day the document is taken to be received` (`Posting facts` OF TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, (Date 5 3 2026)) EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Sections 25 to 28 -- commencement`

-- src:209 | 25 Date on which Acts commence
-- src:210 | An Act or a part of an Act comes into force on the date stated or provided for in the Act.
-- src:211 | If an Act does not state or provide for a commencement date for the Act or part, the Act or part comes into force on the day after Royal assent.
-- src:212 | However, an Act’s Title and commencement sections, and any principal legislation provision, come into force on Royal assent (and no reference in the commencement section includes them).
-- src:213 | In subsection (3), principal legislation provision means a section or part of a section that identifies legislation being amended.
-- (1) the date stated; (2) otherwise the day after Royal assent; (3) the Title, commencement sections and principal legislation provisions on Royal assent.
#ASSERT `the day on which an Act or a part of an Act comes into force` `an ordinary provision` `with the date stated` (JUST (Date 1 7 2025)) `and Royal assent on` (Date 10 3 2025) EQUALS Date 1 7 2025
#ASSERT `the day on which an Act or a part of an Act comes into force` `an ordinary provision` `with the date stated` NOTHING `and Royal assent on` (Date 10 3 2025) EQUALS Date 11 3 2025
#ASSERT `the day on which an Act or a part of an Act comes into force` `the Title` `with the date stated` (JUST (Date 1 7 2025)) `and Royal assent on` (Date 10 3 2025) EQUALS Date 10 3 2025
#ASSERT `the day on which an Act or a part of an Act comes into force` `a commencement section` `with the date stated` (JUST (Date 1 7 2025)) `and Royal assent on` (Date 10 3 2025) EQUALS Date 10 3 2025
#ASSERT `the day on which an Act or a part of an Act comes into force` `a principal legislation provision` `with the date stated` NOTHING `and Royal assent on` (Date 10 3 2025) EQUALS Date 10 3 2025

-- src:214 | 26 Date on which secondary legislation commences
-- src:215 | Secondary legislation or a part of any secondary legislation comes into force on the date stated or provided for in the legislation.
-- src:216 | If it does not state or provide for that date, the legislation or part comes into force on the day after it is published or first made available (whether under Part 3 or otherwise as required by law).
-- src:217 | However, the secondary legislation’s Title and commencement provisions, and any provision identifying the principal legislation amended, come into force on its making (and no reference in the commencement provision includes them), even if it is not yet published.
-- Secondary legislation: the date stated; otherwise the day after it is published or first made available; the Title, commencement provisions and the principal-legislation provision on its making.
#ASSERT `the day on which secondary legislation or a part of it comes into force` `an ordinary provision` `with the date stated` (JUST (Date 1 7 2025)) `made on` (Date 10 3 2025) `and first published or made available on` (JUST (Date 20 3 2025)) EQUALS JUST (Date 1 7 2025)
#ASSERT `the day on which secondary legislation or a part of it comes into force` `an ordinary provision` `with the date stated` NOTHING `made on` (Date 10 3 2025) `and first published or made available on` (JUST (Date 20 3 2025)) EQUALS JUST (Date 21 3 2025)
#ASSERT `the day on which secondary legislation or a part of it comes into force` `an ordinary provision` `with the date stated` NOTHING `made on` (Date 10 3 2025) `and first published or made available on` NOTHING EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the day on which secondary legislation or a part of it comes into force` `the Title` `with the date stated` (JUST (Date 1 7 2025)) `made on` (Date 10 3 2025) `and first published or made available on` NOTHING EQUALS JUST (Date 10 3 2025)

-- src:218 | 27 Time at which legislation commences
-- src:219 | Legislation comes into force—
-- src:220 | at the start of the day on which it comes into force (unless paragraph (b) or (c) applies); or
-- src:221 | if it is expressed to come into force from a particular day, at the start of the next day; or
-- src:222 | if it states or provides for a particular time of commencement on a date on which it comes into force, at that time.
-- (a) the start of the day; (b) "from a particular day": the start of the next day; (c) a stated time on the date.
#ASSERT `the moment at which legislation comes into force` (Date 1 7 2025) FALSE FALSE 0 0 EQUALS JUST (`the start of the day` (Date 1 7 2025))
#ASSERT `the moment at which legislation comes into force` (Date 30 6 2025) TRUE FALSE 0 0 EQUALS JUST (`the start of the day` (Date 1 7 2025))
#ASSERT `the moment at which legislation comes into force` (Date 1 7 2025) FALSE TRUE 14 30 EQUALS JUST (`a time on the day` (Date 1 7 2025) 14 30)
-- (b) and (c) together: the text does not say which prevails (fork F3).
#ASSERT `the moment at which legislation comes into force` (Date 30 6 2025) TRUE TRUE 14 30 EQUALS NOTHING

-- src:212 | However, an Act’s Title and commencement sections, and any principal legislation provision, come into force on Royal assent (and no reference in the commencement section includes them).
-- A reference in the commencement section reaches none of the Title, the commencement sections or a principal legislation provision; for an ordinary provision the Act is silent.
#ASSERT `a reference in the commencement section of an Act includes` `the Title` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `a reference in the commencement section of an Act includes` `a commencement section` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `a reference in the commencement section of an Act includes` `a principal legislation provision` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `a reference in the commencement section of an Act includes` `an ordinary provision` EQUALS NOTHING
-- src:217 | However, the secondary legislation’s Title and commencement provisions, and any provision identifying the principal legislation amended, come into force on its making (and no reference in the commencement provision includes them), even if it is not yet published.
#ASSERT `a reference in the commencement provision of secondary legislation includes` `the Title` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `a reference in the commencement provision of secondary legislation includes` `a commencement section` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `a reference in the commencement provision of secondary legislation includes` `a principal legislation provision` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `a reference in the commencement provision of secondary legislation includes` `an ordinary provision` EQUALS NOTHING

-- src:224 | This section applies to legislation expressed to come into force a number of months after a day or an act or event that occurs on a day (date A in the starting month).
-- Section 28 is about months only: the other units give NOTHING (a commencement "10 days after" or "a year after" Royal assent is outside it).
#ASSERT `the day of commencement when expressed as` (`Period length` OF 1, `months`) `after` (Date 1 7 2020) EQUALS JUST (Date 1 8 2020)
#ASSERT `the day of commencement when expressed as` (`Period length` OF 4, `months`) `after` (Date 31 5 2020) EQUALS JUST (Date 30 9 2020)
#ASSERT `the day of commencement when expressed as` (`Period length` OF 10, `days`) `after` (Date 1 7 2020) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the day of commencement when expressed as` (`Period length` OF 2, `weeks`) `after` (Date 1 7 2020) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the day of commencement when expressed as` (`Period length` OF 1, `years`) `after` (Date 1 7 2020) EQUALS NOTHING
-- src:223 | 28 When legislation commences if calculated by number of months
-- src:224 | This section applies to legislation expressed to come into force a number of months after a day or an act or event that occurs on a day (date A in the starting month).
-- src:225 | The legislation comes into force in the month that is that number of months after the starting month,—
-- src:226 | on the day of that month that corresponds to date A; or
-- src:227 | if there is no such day, on the last day of that month.
-- src:228 | This section applies instead of section 56 (on months generally).
-- src:229 | Examples
-- src:230 | ABC Act 2020 states that it comes into force a month after Royal assent. Royal assent is given on 1 July 2020. The Act comes into force on 1 August 2020.
-- src:231 | XYZ Act 2020 states that it comes into force 4 months after Royal assent. Royal assent is given on 31 May 2020. The Act comes into force on 30 September 2020 (because September does not have 31 days).
-- s 28's own examples: 1 month after Royal assent on 1 July 2020 is 1 August 2020; 4 months after 31 May 2020 is 30 September 2020 (September has no 31st).
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` 1 `months after` (Date 1 7 2020) EQUALS JUST (Date 1 8 2020)
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` 4 `months after` (Date 31 5 2020) EQUALS JUST (Date 30 9 2020)
-- s 87's example: a commencement "6 months after Royal assent" with assent on 1 March 2020 is 1 September 2020.
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` 6 `months after` (Date 1 3 2020) EQUALS JUST (Date 1 9 2020)
-- No day is taken off (contrast s 56): 1 month after 31 January 2024 is the last day of February 2024; after 29 February 2024, 12 months is 28 February 2025.
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` 1 `months after` (Date 31 1 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 29 2 2024)
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` 12 `months after` (Date 29 2 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 28 2 2025)
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` 48 `months after` (Date 29 2 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 29 2 2028)
-- Across a year end: 13 months after 31 December 2024 is 31 January 2026.
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` 13 `months after` (Date 31 12 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 31 1 2026)
-- "a number of months" starts at one: nought and a negative number are outside the section.
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` 0 `months after` (Date 1 7 2020) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the day of commencement` (0 MINUS 1) `months after` (Date 1 7 2020) EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 39 -- time of repeal on a particular day`

-- src:290 | 39 Time of repeal of legislation on particular day
-- src:291 | Legislation that is expressed to be repealed on a particular day is repealed at the start of that day.
-- src:292 | Legislation that is expressed to remain in force, or to continue to have effect, until a particular day, remains in force or has effect until the close of that day.
-- Repealed on 1 July 2026: gone from the start of 1 July. Remains in force until 1 July 2026: in force through the close of 1 July.
#ASSERT `legislation expressed to be repealed on` (Date 1 7 2026) `is still in force on` (Date 30 6 2026)
#ASSERT NOT (`legislation expressed to be repealed on` (Date 1 7 2026) `is still in force on` (Date 1 7 2026))
#ASSERT NOT (`legislation expressed to be repealed on` (Date 1 7 2026) `is still in force on` (Date 2 7 2026))
#ASSERT `legislation expressed to remain in force until` (Date 1 7 2026) `is still in force on` (Date 30 6 2026)
#ASSERT `legislation expressed to remain in force until` (Date 1 7 2026) `is still in force on` (Date 1 7 2026)
#ASSERT NOT (`legislation expressed to remain in force until` (Date 1 7 2026) `is still in force on` (Date 2 7 2026))
#ASSERT `the moment of repeal of legislation expressed to be repealed on` (Date 1 7 2026) EQUALS `the start of the day` (Date 1 7 2026)
#ASSERT `the moment until which legislation remains in force when it is expressed to remain in force until` (Date 1 7 2026) EQUALS `the close of the day` (Date 1 7 2026)
-- src:291 | Legislation that is expressed to be repealed on a particular day is repealed at the start of that day.
-- Section 39 has "on" and "until" only. "Repealed from" a day is not covered, so the moment and the question both answer NOTHING (fork F14).
#ASSERT `the moment of repeal of legislation expressed to be repealed from` (Date 1 7 2026) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `legislation expressed to be repealed from` (Date 1 7 2026) `is still in force on` (Date 30 6 2026) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `legislation expressed to be repealed from` (Date 1 7 2026) `is still in force on` (Date 2 7 2026) EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Sections 54 to 56 -- periods`

-- src:380 | 54 When periods start and end
-- src:381 | If legislation refers to a period described in the following way, the period must be calculated according to the following corresponding rule:
-- src:382 | Examples
-- src:383 | Item 1: If the legislation states that a permission begins on the first day of a financial year, the permission is in force on that day.
-- src:384 | Item 2: If the legislation states that a variation of an agreement operates from 30 June, the variation starts to operate on 1 July.
-- src:385 | Item 3: If the legislation states that public notice must be given within 10 days of adopting a constitution and the constitution is adopted on 1 August, the notice must be given by the close of 11 August.
-- src:386 | Item 4: If the legislation states that a right to make a submission ends on 30 June, the submission may still be made on 30 June.
-- src:387 | Item 5: If the legislation states that a permission continues until 30 March, the permission is still in force on 30 March.
-- src:388 | Item 6: If the legislation states that a statement must be filed before the start of the next financial year, the statement must be filed on 30 June, not 1 July.
-- src:389 | Item 7: If the legislation states that the period between sending a draft decision document to submitters for comments and finalising the decision must be at least 10 working days, do not count the day on which the draft is sent or the day on which the decision is finalised.
-- The Items of s 54, each as its own example states it (the year is chosen, the day and month are the Act's).
-- Item 1: "a permission begins on the first day of a financial year": in force on that day (1 July).
#ASSERT `the first day of a period that is` `starting or beginning on the day named` `by reference to` (Date 1 7 2026) EQUALS Date 1 7 2026
-- Item 2: "a variation operates from 30 June": it starts to operate on 1 July.
#ASSERT `the first day of a period that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 30 6 2026) EQUALS Date 1 7 2026
-- Item 3: "within 10 days of adopting a constitution", adopted 1 August: notice by the close of 11 August.
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 10, `days`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 1 8 2026) EQUALS JUST (Date 11 8 2026)
-- Item 4: "a right to make a submission ends on 30 June": the submission may still be made on 30 June.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period that is` `ending on the day named` `by reference to` (Date 30 6 2026) EQUALS Date 30 6 2026
-- Item 5: "a permission continues until 30 March": still in force on 30 March.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period that is` `continuing until the day named` `by reference to` (Date 30 3 2026) EQUALS Date 30 3 2026
-- Item 6: "filed before the start of the next financial year": filed on 30 June, not 1 July.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period that is` `ending before the start of the day named` `by reference to` (Date 1 7 2026) EQUALS Date 30 6 2026

-- Item 7: "at least 10 working days" between sending the draft and finalising the decision, counting neither end day.
-- Draft sent Monday 2 March 2026 (no holidays in March 2026): the working days after it are 3, 4, 5, 6, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13 and 16 March,
-- so the tenth is Monday 16 March, and the earliest day to finalise is Tuesday 17 March; on 16 March only nine days lie between.
#ASSERT `the number of working days strictly between` (Date 2 3 2026) `and` (Date 17 3 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST 10
#ASSERT `the number of working days strictly between` (Date 2 3 2026) `and` (Date 16 3 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST 9
#ASSERT `the earliest day for the second event if at least` 10 `working days must lie between it and the first event on` (Date 2 3 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST (Date 17 3 2026)
-- Draft sent Wednesday 1 April 2026; Good Friday 3 April and Easter Monday 6 April are not working days, so the tenth working day is Friday 17 April and the earliest day is Saturday 18 April.
#ASSERT `the earliest day for the second event if at least` 10 `working days must lie between it and the first event on` (Date 1 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST (Date 18 4 2026)
#ASSERT `the number of working days strictly between` (Date 1 4 2026) `and` (Date 18 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST 10
-- Adjacent days have nothing between them; no calendar for the year gives NOTHING.
#ASSERT `the number of working days strictly between` (Date 2 3 2026) `and` (Date 3 3 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST 0
#ASSERT `the number of working days strictly between` (Date 2 3 2026) `and` (Date 17 3 2026) `according to` EMPTY EQUALS NOTHING

-- src:392 | 56 Calculating periods of months (except for commencement of legislation)
-- src:393 | A reference to 1 or more months in legislation is a reference to a period calculated as follows:
-- src:394 | the period starts at the start of the relevant day in the month; and
-- src:395 | the relevant number of months must then be counted to find the ending month; and
-- src:396 | the period ends immediately before the corresponding day in the ending month or (if there is no such day) at the close of the last day of the ending month.
-- src:397 | Examples
-- src:398 | If the legislation refers to a period of 1 month starting on 15 February, the period starts on 15 February and ends at the close of 14 March (because March has a corresponding day).
-- src:399 | If the legislation refers to a period of 6 months starting on 31 October, the period starts on 31 October and ends at the close of 30 April (because April does not have 31 days).
-- src:400 | If the legislation refers to a period of 3 months after the date on which notice is sent and notice is sent on 2 August, the period starts on 3 August (under item 2 in section 54) and ends at the close of 2 November.
-- src:401 | This section does not apply to commencement of legislation (see section 28).
-- s 56's examples: 1 month starting on 15 February ends at the close of 14 March; 6 months starting on 31 October ends at the close of 30 April (April has no 31st).
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 1 `starting on` (Date 15 2 2026) EQUALS JUST (Date 14 3 2026)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 6 `starting on` (Date 31 10 2025) EQUALS JUST (Date 30 4 2026)
-- s 56's third example: 3 months after the date notice is sent (2 August) starts on 3 August (item 2) and ends at the close of 2 November.
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 3, `months`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 2 8 2025) EQUALS JUST (Date 2 11 2025)
-- Corresponding day exists in the ending month: the period ends the day before it. It does not, or is the 1st: see each case.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 1 `starting on` (Date 1 1 2025) EQUALS JUST (Date 31 1 2025)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 2 `starting on` (Date 15 12 2025) EQUALS JUST (Date 14 2 2026)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 1 `starting on` (Date 31 1 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 29 2 2024)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 1 `starting on` (Date 30 1 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 29 2 2024)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 1 `starting on` (Date 29 1 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 28 2 2024)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 1 `starting on` (Date 29 1 2023) EQUALS JUST (Date 28 2 2023)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 2 `starting on` (Date 31 12 2025) EQUALS JUST (Date 28 2 2026)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 12 `starting on` (Date 29 2 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 28 2 2025)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 48 `starting on` (Date 29 2 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 28 2 2028)
-- "1 or more months": nought and a negative number are outside the section.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` 0 `starting on` (Date 15 2 2026) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of months` (0 MINUS 2) `starting on` (Date 15 2 2026) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Periods of days: the starting day is the first of the N days.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of days` 10 `starting on` (Date 2 8 2026) EQUALS JUST (Date 11 8 2026)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of days` 1 `starting on` (Date 2 8 2026) EQUALS JUST (Date 2 8 2026)
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of days` 0 `starting on` (Date 2 8 2026) EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Seven worked cases (brief) -- answers decided from the text before running`

-- 1-3. Years: the Act does not define a year and s 56 speaks only of months, so NOTHING.
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 3, `years`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 16 7 2025) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 5, `years`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 15 1 2025) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 5, `years`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 1 9 2025) EQUALS NOTHING
-- 7. one year from 29 February 2024: silent as well.
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 1, `years`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 29 2 2024) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Weeks are not defined either.
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 2, `weeks`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS NOTHING
-- 4. thirty days from 1 January 2026: starts 2 January (Item 2), the thirtieth day is 31 January 2026 (a Saturday).
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 30, `days`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS JUST (Date 31 1 2026)
-- 5. two months from 31 December 2025: starts 1 January 2026, ending month March, the 1st exists: the close of 28 February 2026 (a Saturday).
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 2, `months`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 31 12 2025) EQUALS JUST (Date 28 2 2026)
-- 6. one month from 31 January 2024: starts 1 February, ending month March: the close of 29 February 2024 (a Thursday).
#ASSERT `the last day of a described period` (`Period length` OF 1, `months`) `that is` `from or after the day or the event named` `by reference to` (Date 31 1 2024) EQUALS JUST (Date 29 2 2024)

§ `Anniversaries (section 2(1)(c) and Schedule 1)`

-- src:12 | to the extent not brought into force earlier, on the fifth anniversary of the date on which the Secondary Legislation Act 2021 receives the Royal assent.
-- src:1162 | the first anniversary of the publication commencement date (unless paragraph (b) or (c) applies); or
-- No definition of anniversary and none of a year: NOTHING for every date, 29 February included (fork F12).
#ASSERT `the anniversary of` (Date 29 2 2024) `after` 1 `years` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the anniversary of` (Date 5 1 2026) `after` 1 `years` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the anniversary of` (Date 5 1 2026) `after` 5 `years` EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 55 -- extension when the day or the last day is not a working day`

-- src:390 | 55 Extension for doing thing if day or last day is not working day
-- src:391 | A thing that, under legislation, must or may be done on a particular day or within a limited period of time may, if that day or the last day of that period is not a working day, be done on the next working day.
-- 30 January 2026 is a Friday and a working day: no extension. 31 January 2026 is a Saturday: the next working day is Monday 2 February.
#ASSERT `the last day on which the thing may be done under section 55` (Date 30 1 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST (Date 30 1 2026)
#ASSERT `the last day on which the thing may be done under section 55` (Date 31 1 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST (Date 2 2 2026)
#ASSERT `the last day on which the thing may be done under section 55` (Date 1 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST (Date 2 2 2026)
-- Good Friday 3 April 2026: the weekend and Easter Monday follow, so Tuesday 7 April.
#ASSERT `the last day on which the thing may be done under section 55` (Date 3 4 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST (Date 7 4 2026)
-- A thing due by Saturday 31 January 2026 "may" be done on Monday 2 February, but not on Tuesday 3 February; and it can still be done earlier.
#ASSERT `the thing due by` (Date 31 1 2026) `may be done on` (Date 2 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `the thing due by` (Date 31 1 2026) `may be done on` (Date 3 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `the thing due by` (Date 31 1 2026) `may be done on` (Date 30 1 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 55 reaches a period of months too ("a limited period of time"): the last day of 2 months from 31 December 2025 is Saturday 28 February 2026, so Monday 2 March 2026.
#ASSERT `the last day on which the thing may be done under section 55` (Date 28 2 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS JUST (Date 2 3 2026)
-- The worked case: 31 January 2026 gives Monday 2 February 2026; the thirtieth day of the other reading, 30 January 2026, needs no extension.
-- Across a year end the search needs the next year's calendar: with only 2026 supplied there is NOTHING; with both, the first working day is Tuesday 5 January 2027.
#ASSERT `the last day on which the thing may be done under section 55` (Date 25 12 2026) `according to` `the calendar for 2026` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the last day on which the thing may be done under section 55` (Date 25 12 2026) `according to` `the calendars for 2026 and 2027` EQUALS JUST (Date 5 1 2027)
-- The last day of one month from 31 January 2024 is Thursday 29 February 2024, a working day (calendar for 2024): no extension.
#ASSERT `the last day on which the thing may be done under section 55` (Date 29 2 2024) `according to` `the calendar for 2024` EQUALS JUST (Date 29 2 2024)

§ `Section 57 -- determining time`

-- src:402 | 57 Determining time generally
-- src:403 | A reference to a point of time in legislation is to time for general purposes in New Zealand as determined by sections 3 and 4 of the Time Act 1974 (which provides for New Zealand standard time and New Zealand daylight time).
-- 31 March 2026 at 23:30 UTC (minute 1410) with New Zealand daylight time (+13 h = 780 minutes) is 12:30 on 1 April 2026; 30 June at midnight UTC with standard time (+12 h = 720) is 12:00 the same day.
#ASSERT `the New Zealand time of` (Date 31 3 2026) `at` 1410 `with offset` 780 EQUALS `a time on the day` (Date 1 4 2026) 12 30
#ASSERT `the New Zealand time of` (Date 30 6 2026) `at` 0 `with offset` 720 EQUALS `a time on the day` (Date 30 6 2026) 12 0

§ `Section 58 -- distance`

GIVETH A Place
`the origin` MEANS Place OF 0, 0, 0

GIVETH A Place
`a point 3 east, 4 north and 100 above` MEANS Place OF 3, 4, 100

-- src:404 | 58 Determining measurements of distance
-- src:405 | A reference to a distance means a distance measured in a straight line on a horizontal plane.
-- A straight line on a horizontal plane: the 100 metres of height do not count, so the distance is 5.
#ASSERT `the square of the straight-line horizontal distance between` `the origin` `and` `a point 3 east, 4 north and 100 above` EQUALS 25
#ASSERT `the distance between` `the origin` `and` `a point 3 east, 4 north and 100 above` `is no more than` 5 `metres`
#ASSERT NOT (`the distance between` `the origin` `and` `a point 3 east, 4 north and 100 above` `is no more than` 4 `metres`)

§ `What the Act says about day, week, month and year`

-- src:80 | sitting day means a sitting day of the House of Representatives.
#ASSERT `what the Act says of` `a day` EQUALS `The Act is silent`
#ASSERT `what the Act says of` `a week` EQUALS `The Act is silent`
#ASSERT `what the Act says of` `a year` EQUALS `The Act is silent`
#ASSERT `what the Act says of` `a month` EQUALS `Defined by the Act` "a calendar month" "section 13"
#ASSERT `what the Act says of` `a sitting day` EQUALS `Defined by the Act` "a sitting day of the House of Representatives" "section 5(1)"

§ `Section 17(5) -- electronic communications`

-- src:183 | See also Part 4 of the Contract and Commercial Law Act 2017, which provides for the deemed timing and receipt of electronic communications.
#ASSERT `the deemed timing and receipt of an electronic communication is governed by Part 4 of the Contract and Commercial Law Act 2017` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `the deemed timing and receipt of an electronic communication is governed by Part 4 of the Contract and Commercial Law Act 2017` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
